 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Y is high only when A and B are both high; a low on either input makes Y low. The second form, one bar over A + B, is a misprint for Y = NOT(Ā + B̄)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overvoltage-tolerant inputs and down-translation",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "A and B accept up to 5.5 V at every supply voltage from 1.65 V to 5.5 V, so a 5 V signal can drive the gate while it runs from a lower VCC and the output then swings only to that VCC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Partial power-down (Ioff)",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "With VCC at 0 V the Ioff circuit turns the output off, so voltage on the inputs or output drives no damaging current back into the part (Ioff is ±10 µA at most with 5.5 V applied). This allows live insertion, partial power-down and back-drive."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output drive",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The CMOS output sinks and sources equally, 24 mA at 3.3 V, enough for LEDs or several logic inputs, and the gate is suited to signals up to 100 MHz. Into a light load its edges are fast, so trace routing and loading affect ringing. Bus contention, the output fighting another driver, can draw currents above the absolute maximum ratings."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Design limits",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Input edge rates are bounded by Δt/Δv and input levels by VIH and VIL; the inputs tolerate up to the 5.5 V VI maximum at every valid VCC. Output current is bounded by the 50 mA IO rating per output and by the 100 mA rating through VCC or GND for the part, and the output is not to be pulled above VCC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply and bypassing",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The part runs from any supply within the recommended VCC range. A device with a single VCC pin takes a 0.1 µF bypass capacitor; a device with several VCC pins takes 0.01 µF or 0.022 µF at each. Parallel capacitors of different values, commonly 0.1 µF with 1 µF, suppress noise over different frequency bands. The bypass capacitor goes as close to the supply pin as possible."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused inputs",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Never leave an input open: a floating CMOS input settles at an undefined level and the gate's state becomes undefined. Tie any unused input to VCC or to GND, whichever fits the logic."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply current against frequency",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Dynamic supply current rises with switching frequency and VCC; Cpd is 21 pF at 1.8 V up to 31 pF at 5 V (f = 10 MHz, 25 °C). An input held at VCC – 0.6 V instead of a rail adds up to 500 µA (ΔICC)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD and latch-up",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The pins withstand ±2000 V HBM (AEC Q100-002) and ±1000 V CDM (AEC Q100-011). Latch-up immunity exceeds 100 mA per JESD 78, Class II."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Automotive qualification and grades",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part is AEC-Q100 qualified, device temperature grade 1 (−40 °C to 125 °C ambient). Q-suffix parts are rated −40 °C to 125 °C; the I-suffix part (SN74LVC1G08IDCKRQ1) is rated −40 °C to 85 °C. Commercial (SN74LVC1G08) and Enhanced Product (SN74LVC1G08-EP) versions of the gate also exist."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package and mounting",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "All packages are MSL level 1 with a 260 °C peak reflow. DBV and DCK body sizes exclude mold flash, protrusions and gate burrs, which add up to 0.25 mm per side. DBV and DCK bodies may carry a small support pin on the side opposite pin 2, or none. DBV follows JEDEC MO-178; DCK follows JEDEC MO-203 except that its lead width does not comply."
  }
 ],
